    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I changed and why:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, and everything derived from it follows automatically.\n\n**`CustomerCode.java`** (`rest/controller/v1`) \u2014 rewrote `of(...)` to return `region(owner) + \"-\" + hash8(telephone + lastName)`:\n- `region(owner)` derives the region from the postcode (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099), falling back to the fixed city table, else `UNKNOWN`.\n- `hash8(...)` takes the first 8 upper-case hex characters of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName` (the telephone is already stored in E.164-normalized form).\n- Sequence numbers are gone.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.deriveLocality`** (`mapper`) \u2014 locality now reads the REGION segment off the `customerCode`, falling back to `LocalityResolver` only when no code is present.\n\nThe membership number, its Luhn check digit, and the create audit record are all built by reading `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so they moved to the new identity with **no code change** in those files (verified in the run: `AUDIT - ... customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`).\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n- I kept `of(Owner, Collection)`'s signature identical (the `existing` collection is simply no longer used), so the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` class \u2014 including `addOwner` and its audit line \u2014 was **not touched at all**. That avoided paying its whole-class `WMC_other`.\n- Only **2 files** changed. `CustomerCode` is a tiny cohesive class (low surrounding complexity), and the mapper edit is a single small `default` method. The membership/check-digit/audit behaviours ride along unchanged.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
